PLACES=[
N('jackson-hotel-paola-ks','Jackson Hotel','Paola, Kansas','ks','hotel','standing',38.57287,-94.87990,'exact','view','drive','','1921',
  'The three-storey red brick Jackson Hotel at 139 West Peoria Street was built in 1921 to a design by the architect George P. Washburn. A hotel had stood on the site since 1863, and this was the third. From 1937 to 1969 Paola directories listed it as one of two hotels in town, and from 1969 as the only one. It stood empty by 1992 until a redevelopment effort began in 2006.',
  'A plain Commercial-style hotel with a full-length porch across the ground floor and an entry with a transom and sidelights, meant to look like a large house. What it is used for now and what is left inside were not checked.',
  'A 1921 brick hotel on the third hotel site in town.',
  'On Peoria Street, a block west of the square. Look from the public street; present use and hours were not checked.',
  [W('Jackson Hotel (Paola, Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
N('miami-county-courthouse-paola-ks','Miami County Courthouse','Paola, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.57087,-94.87866,'exact','view','drive','','1898–1899',
  'The county courthouse stands on Paola\'s square at Miami and Silver Streets and has housed county government since it was built in 1898 and 1899. George P. Washburn designed it in a Victorian style with Romanesque Revival details. It was listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'Towers at each corner (the south-west one was once a clock tower and has no clock now), arched upper windows trimmed with stone, a band of stone running round the walls, and east and west porches with brick columns.',
  'A Romanesque courthouse by the architect who also drew half the buildings round the square.',
  'A working county building on the public square; look from the street. Opening hours were not checked, and the interior was not checked.',
  [W('Miami County Courthouse (Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
N('paola-city-hall-ks','Paola City Hall','Paola, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.57226,-94.87684,'exact','view','drive','','1921',
  'Paola City Hall at 19 East Peoria Street was completed in 1921 to a design by George P. Washburn, of Ottawa, Kansas, late in his career. Where his many courthouses are Romanesque, this one is a quieter early twentieth-century Classical Revival. It was built to hold the city offices, the police and the fire trucks, and the Kansas Historical Society says all three still work from it. It is on the Kansas state register (2008).',
  'A brick front with light quoining at the corners, stone around the doors and windows, and an eight-sided cupola on classical columns. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1921 city hall that still houses the fire trucks.',
  'A working city building; look from Peoria Street. Public hours were not checked.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
N('paola-free-library-ks','Paola Free Library','Paola, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.57218,-94.87637,'exact','open','drive','','1905–1906',
  'The Paola Free Library at 101 East Peoria Street was designed by George P. Washburn and finished in 1906 on a corner lot across the street from City Hall. The Kansas Historical Society calls it Richardsonian Romanesque and lists it on the state register (2008). A waymarking entry says it was built in 1905 and opened in 1906; no source read here says who paid for it.',
  'A masonry building with a round-arched main entrance framed by Corinthian columns, a rounded tower with a conical roof, quoins at the corners and original wooden windows, according to the state register summary.',
  'A little Romanesque library with a round tower.',
  'A public library you may walk into. Listings of its hours disagree and none was the library\'s own page, so the hours are not checked; telephone 913-259-3655 is given in one listing.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR],['Waymarking: Paola Free Library','https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/wm6203_Library_Paola_Free_Library_Paola_Kansas']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('miami-county-mercantile-paola-ks','Miami County Mercantile Company building','Paola, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.57115,-94.87784,'exact','view','drive','','1904',
  'The building at 121 South Pearl Street was put up in the summer of 1904 by the Miami County Mercantile Company and opened that October as a household furnishings store, described then as equal to any in Topeka. A doctor moved his practice into it at the same time. In 1956 the Miami County Publishing Company bought it and renovated it; the storefront and some first-floor finishes changed but still read as Commercial Style, and local newspapers were printed there. It was listed on the National Register in 2021.',
  'A Commercial Style downtown store building whose altered storefront still shows its style. Present use and interior not checked.',
  'A 1904 furniture store that later printed the county newspaper.',
  'Downtown Paola, a block south of the square. Look from the street; use and hours not checked.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
N('ursuline-academy-auditorium-paola-ks','Ursuline Academy auditorium (Paola Community Center)','Paola, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.56982,-94.86792,'site','open','drive','','1916',
  'The Ursuline Sisters ran their academy at Paola as a school from 1896 to 1924, then as a junior college, which closed in 1957; the high school was discontinued in 1971. The auditorium was built in 1916 as a late addition to the academy, in Colonial Revival style, according to the descriptions of the National Register form quoted on Wikimedia Commons. A parish history said it was one of the finest auditoriums west of the Mississippi. Commons gives its present name as the Paola Community Center, and says a 2009 renovation added an elevator. It is on the National Register of Historic Places; the listing date was not checked.',
  'A large red brick building of three storeys with light stone trim, arched ground-floor windows and a cross over the south entrance, which was the original main door. Whether the auditorium inside survives was not checked.',
  'A convent school\'s grand brick auditorium, now the town community center.',
  'Now the Paola Community Center, a public building; its hours were not read from the city\'s page, so they are not checked. The pin is on the building seen on the aerial.',
  [['Ursuline Sisters: the academy at Paola','https://ursulinesmsj.org/ursuline-academy-paola-kansas/'],['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('osawatomie-congregational-church-ks','Congregational Church (Old Stone Church), Osawatomie','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.50038,-94.95097,'exact','view','drive','','1858–1861',
  'The Rev. Samuel Adair, who was married to John Brown\'s half-sister Florella, organised this Congregational church in 1856 and built the stone church at 315 Sixth Street between 1858 and 1861 with his son, using money raised in the East. It was one of the first three Congregational churches organised in Kansas, and it was dedicated on July 14, 1861. The congregation first met in the Adair cabin, which is now in John Brown Memorial Park. It was listed on the National Register in 2013.',
  'A one-room stone church with Greek Revival and Gothic Revival touches. A visitor listing says it is no longer a regular congregation and is rented for events and has been called an all-faith chapel since 1963; interior not checked.',
  'The stone church an abolitionist preacher and his son built by hand.',
  'On Sixth Street in town. Listings say "call for info" and give 913-755-4384; no open hours were found, so look from the street unless arranged.',
  [W('Congregational Church (Osawatomie, Kansas)'),['VisitKC: Old Stone Church','https://visitkc.com/business-detail/old-stone-church'],['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('john-brown-cabin-osawatomie-ks','John Brown Museum and the Adair cabin','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.4989,-94.9594,'site','open','drive','','1850s',
  'The log cabin of the Rev. Samuel Adair and Florella Brown Adair, John Brown\'s half-sister, is where Brown stayed during his twenty months in Kansas. His son Frederick was killed nearby in the first Battle of Osawatomie. The cabin survived the attack, and in 1911 Kansas named the battlefield John Brown Memorial Park and moved the cabin to its present site. A stone building put up in 1928 now encloses it, beside a museum run by the Kansas Historical Society.',
  'The original cabin, kept under the 1928 stone shelter, with Adair family furnishings and Civil War objects in the museum displays.',
  'A frontier cabin where John Brown lived, still under its 1928 stone roof.',
  'State historic site in John Brown Memorial Park, run by the Kansas Historical Society; admission free, donations welcome, group tours by appointment, telephone 913-755-4384. Its page says street closure and construction in the park affect parking.',
  [W('John Brown Museum (Osawatomie, Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society: plan your visit','https://www.kansashistory.gov/p/john-brown-museum-plan-your-visit/11854']],hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed state holidays; free',brick='no'),
N('soldiers-monument-osawatomie-ks','Soldiers\' Monument, Osawatomie','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','curio','standing',38.4981,-94.9566,'site','view','walk','','1877',
  'The monument at Main and Ninth Streets was put up in 1877, two blocks from where five men died defending the town on August 30, 1856. It names John Brown and five others, including his son Frederick. The bodies of four of the men were moved here in 1860, and a local association bought an 11-foot shaft of Vermont marble for $275 from the Hanway Brothers of Lane, Kansas. Several thousand people came to the dedication, and later battle anniversaries drew speakers including Vice President Charles Fairbanks in 1906. National Register, 2012.',
  'An 11-foot marble shaft naming the men of the 1856 battle.',
  'A marble obelisk over graves from John Brown\'s battle.',
  'On the corner of Main and Ninth Streets in town, at the roadside on a street corner, so it can be seen from the public way; who owns the corner was not checked. The register coordinate puts the pin on the corner, not on the shaft.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('asylum-bridge-osawatomie-ks','Asylum Bridge (First Street Bridge)','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.5052,-94.9420,'site','view','walk','','1905',
  'The Asylum Bridge carried First Street over the Marais des Cygnes River to the south entrance of the state hospital. The Kansas City Bridge Company built it for $4,800 and finished it on December 22, 1905. It is a single pin-connected truss span 219 feet long and 16.5 feet wide, with gas lights at each end, and it is the only known bridge in the United States with a reverse Parker truss, whose top chord curves down rather than up. It was closed to cars in 1979 and to everyone in 1998, and is on the National Register (1990).',
  'The iron truss itself, with towers at each end that make it look cantilevered. It is shut to all traffic.',
  'The only reverse-Parker truss bridge in the country, closed since 1998.',
  'Seen from the river bank or the road near the hospital grounds; it is closed to traffic and walkers, and the land beside it was not checked for ownership.',
  [W('Asylum Bridge'),['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('careys-ford-bridge-osawatomie-ks','Carey\'s Ford Bridge','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.5207,-95.0336,'site','view','drive','','1909',
  'This camelback through-truss crosses the Marais des Cygnes River a few miles south-east of Rantoul. The Kansas City Bridge Company won the contract on December 8, 1908 with a $6,885 bid, after a local row about the number and place of new bridges, and finished it in 1909. It is the oldest camelback through truss left in Kansas. A 2014 inspection called its condition serious, and the county closed it on July 7, 2021 until further notice. National Register, 1990.',
  'A steel camelback truss with a five-slope top chord and two pony truss approach spans, 37 feet above the river, with pinned joints already old-fashioned by 1909.',
  'The oldest camelback truss bridge in Kansas, closed since 2021.',
  'On a county road; look from the approach. The bridge is closed and the pin uses the register coordinate, so it may be off the span.',
  [W('Carey\'s Ford Bridge'),['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('creamery-bridge-osawatomie-ks','Creamery Bridge (Marsh arch), Osawatomie','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.5031,-94.955,'site','view','drive','','about 1930',
  'This concrete bridge over the Marais des Cygnes at the north edge of town is a Marsh "rainbow arch", a design patented by James Marsh in 1911 in which the arches run below the deck and are fixed into massive piers. A 140-foot centre arch has an 80-foot arch at each end, 346 feet 6 inches in all, and the piers stand on rock about 50 feet down. The National Register listing (1983) dates it to 1931, while other listings say 1930; the sources do not agree.',
  'Three concrete arches under the roadway, best seen from the river bank. Whether the road is still open to traffic was not checked.',
  'A concrete rainbow-arch bridge on piers sunk to rock.',
  'On a county road at the north edge of town; look from the roadside. Land beside the river not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Creamery Bridge (83000434)',NR%'83000434'],['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('pottawatomie-creek-bridge-osawatomie-ks','Pottawatomie Creek Bridge (Marsh arch), Osawatomie','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.4853,-94.9506,'site','view','drive','','1932',
  'A second Marsh rainbow-arch bridge, half a mile south of Osawatomie. It is 370 feet long, with a 120-foot centre arch, two 80-foot arches and two 40-foot approach spans, 24 feet wide and 27 feet high at the tallest arch. The form says it cost $49,000 and used 1,500 yards of concrete and 313,000 pounds of steel. The Osawatomie Graphic-News reported it nearly finished on June 9, 1932. The two smaller arches show where overhead struts were removed. National Register, 1983.',
  'Five concrete spans over the creek; best seen from the bank. Whether it carries traffic now was not checked.',
  'The bigger of Osawatomie\'s two concrete rainbow bridges.',
  'On a county road south of town; look from the roadside. The register coordinate may not sit on the span.',
  [['National Register form, Pottawatomie Creek Bridge (83000433)',NR%'83000433'],['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('william-mills-house-osawatomie-ks','William Mills House','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.50278,-94.94230,'exact','view','drive','','1902–1904',
  'William McKee Mills (1851–1929), who worked in oil and gas drilling in south-eastern Kansas from 1884, had this house built at 212 First Street at the height of his career for $49,000. The 1986 National Register form calls it the largest and most ornate house then standing in Osawatomie. It is a late Queen Anne design.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey balloon-frame house in cedar lap siding on a rusticated limestone foundation, with a cross-gabled hipped roof and brick chimneys with corbelled pots. Interior not checked and its use now is not known.',
  'A $49,000 oilman\'s mansion from 1900s Osawatomie.',
  'Look from First Street; it appears to be a private property and its present use was not checked.',
  [['National Register form, William Mills House (86003291)',NR%'86003291'],['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='some'),
N('new-lancaster-general-store-ks','New Lancaster General Store','New Lancaster, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.4626,-94.7328,'area','view','drive','','1903',
  'Members of New Lancaster Grange No. 223 built the store in 1903 and ran it as a farmers\' co-operative until they sold it in 1928, when it became Smith Brothers General Merchandise. Frank Smith kept it until he retired in 1968. It is a wooden false-front store of the kind common in small Kansas towns of 1900, still with its original storefront, and was listed on the National Register in 2013.',
  'The false-front wooden storefront. Interior and present use were not checked.',
  'A grange co-operative store from 1903 in a vanished crossroads.',
  'In the rural hamlet of New Lancaster in south-east Miami County; the register address is 36688 New Lancaster Road. Ownership and whether it can be visited were not checked; look from the road.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('new-lancaster-grange-hall-ks','New Lancaster Grange Hall No. 223','New Lancaster, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.46202,-94.7324,'area','view','drive','','1885',
  'The hall was built in 1885 as a house of worship for the Beulah Baptist Church and used for services until 1901, when New Lancaster Grange No. 223 bought it for its meetings. The farmers had organised the grange in 1873. Falling membership closed the hall and it was sold in 2005. The same grange ran the general store nearby from 1903 to 1928. National Register, 2013.',
  'A plain vernacular country meeting house. What materials it is built of, what is inside and who owns it now were not checked.',
  'A country Baptist church that became a farmers\' grange hall.',
  'On West 367th Street at New Lancaster; look from the road. Ownership not checked.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Miami County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('osawatomie-state-hospital-ks','Osawatomie State Hospital','Osawatomie, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',38.51025,-94.94011,'area','view','drive','','1863–',
  'The Kansas legislature set up the state\'s first mental hospital here in 1863, on land given by the Rev. Samuel Adair, and it opened in 1866. It is the oldest psychiatric hospital still working in Kansas, and is listed as a Kirkbride-plan hospital. The first building was a renovated two-storey farmhouse, "The Lodge", for 10 or 12 patients. A fire on March 8, 1880 badly damaged the main building, though the wings were saved. The Knapp Building came in 1892 and the Adair Building in 1895.',
  'The campus is a working hospital and the oldest buildings that survive on it were not checked.',
  'The oldest working asylum in Kansas, on land a John Brown relative gave.',
  'A state hospital with patients: look only from public roads and do not enter the grounds without permission. Any tours were not checked.',
  [W('Osawatomie State Hospital')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
